; 09-qed.kurt

; `qed` closes the most recent `proof` block (08-proof.kurt).  It takes
; everything derived inside the block, tries once more to derive the shown
; goal, and -- if that succeeds -- adds the *goal* (not the intermediate
; steps) to the theory, at the level where it was `show`n.

bool A, B

use A implies B
use A

show B
proof
    A          ; re-deriving something we already had is fine
qed

B              ; `B` is now available outside the block

; If the goal cannot be derived, `qed` raises a `ProofError` instead of
; closing the block -- try commenting out `use A implies B` above and rerun
; this file to see it fail.

; In a *file*, `qed` must match the indentation of the `show`/`proof` it
; closes exactly -- dedenting is how Kurt knows which (and how many nested)
; blocks to close.  The interactive shell is more forgiving: there, a single
; `qed` can close several nested blocks at once.
